{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run2:inflation:bayes","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:5579fe1203b3685c7acf3a82c3c3e804bdf60132206b176200e559605a870e69","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"binary","positiveOutcomeId":"yes","probability":0.32},"questionHash":"sha256:a11e957a2fbf9e72130705a8815d67b5a6c66c3444a8332ee92cdcb4248068e1","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq2","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"PRIOR: Historian's 14 (conjunct A = 0.27, conjunct B = 0.58, rho -0.20). This is the CQ where my seat moves furthest, and the move is driven mostly by a STRUCTURAL ERROR in the prior rather than by evidence — I report that distinction because it is the honest accounting.\n\nCONJUNCT A (OECD-38 GDP-weighted core >=2.50%/yr, 2027-2036). STRUCTURAL CORRECTION FIRST (Fermi's identity, admitted as structure): the Historian's composite is a US-style core basket GDP-blended with euro area and Japan, and it OMITS the five high-inflation OECD-38 members. Fermi's build: US 0.45x2.25 + EA 0.22x2.0 + JP 0.09x1.6 + UK 0.055x2.5 + CA 0.04x2.1 + KR 0.035x2.0 + AU 0.025x2.5 + (Turkiye, Mexico, Chile, Colombia, Costa Rica) 0.085 x ~6.5% = 2.503%. My own check: at ~8.8% PPP weight averaging ~7%, 0.088x7 + 0.912x2.1 = 0.616 + 1.915 = 2.53%. The locked accounting basis is constant 2025 USD PPP, which RAISES the Turkiye/Mexico weights further. The Historian's 2.14% composite is a correct answer to a different question (core OECD). Prior conjunct A therefore resets from 0.27 to 0.50 — the aggregate sits ON the threshold, so a coin flip is the structurally honest starting odds (1.00).\nEVIDENCE CLUSTER E1 (hard administrative, treated as ONE signal because the components are readings of a single latent state — multiplying them separately is this seat's classic failure): US core PCE +3.1% y/y Sept 2026 (BEA, 29 Oct 2026), +0.3% m/m, \"the same pace as a revised reading for August\"; US core PCE has printed >=2.5% every year 2021-2026; Germany Oct 2026 core ex food/energy +2.1% (Destatis), Spain core 3.1% (INE), euro headline 3.8% Sept 2026 (secondary, and the Scout notes sources \"conflict on the timing of the euro area's latest data\"). Cluster LR = P(window-open core at 2.1-3.1% across the big weights | 10-yr avg >=2.50%) / P(same | <2.50%) = 2.2:1. Justified above 2:1 by services-inflation autocorrelation plus the front-loading arithmetic: 2027-2030 at ~2.9% leaves 2031-2036 needing only >=2.23%.\nEVIDENCE E2 (semi-independent): Census BTOS national AI use 19.8% (3 May 2026) with Information 39.7% and Finance 33.9%, \"neither sector shifted significantly since December\", plus the Nov 2025 wording break from \"producing goods or services\" to \"any business function\" (\"Part of the apparent jump reflects that wording change rather than pure adoption growth\"). This is evidence AGAINST a large AI-disinflation leg in 2031-36, hence FOR conjunct A. LR 1.4 raw, DISCOUNTED to 1.2 (survey, not administrative; a six-month flat read is thin).\nEVIDENCE E3 DISCOUNTED TO 1.0 AND FLAGGED: US residential electricity rates +7.3% y/y Apr 2025-Apr 2026; EIA STEO residential average 18.2 c/kWh in 2026, commercial sales +2.2% in 2026 and +5.3% in 2027 vs residential ~0.5%/yr, commercial surpassing residential \"for the first time on record in 2027\"; PJM's independent market monitor attributing \"roughly 63% of the 2025-2026 auction price surge to AI-focused data center demand\", against which EPRI \"found that between 2015 and 2024, data center growth in the United States modestly reduced average retail electricity rates, which conflicts with the PJM findings\" and PolitiFact rated a 267%-bill claim \"Mostly False\"; utilities also \"cite higher fuel prices for generation and expenses for bolstering the transmission grid\". Household electricity and gas are EXCLUDED from US core CPI, core PCE and euro HICP core, so the pass-through to the resolution measure is second-round business cost only, AND the attribution is contested. LR unjustifiable above 1.3 in either direction → 1.0. This is the single most emotionally compelling signal in my evidence set and I am spending none of it.\nPosterior odds = 1.00 x 2.2 x 1.2 = 2.64; applied an independence haircut of 0.62 for residual overlap between E1/E2 and the structural correction already embedded in the 0.50 → net effective LR 1.63 → odds 1.63 → CONJUNCT A = 0.62.\n\nCONJUNCT B (China headline CPI <1.50%/yr, 2027-2036). Prior 0.58 (odds 1.381), from the Historian's post-property-bust aging East Asia class: Japan's rolling 10-yr CPI averages below 1.5% continuously from the window ending 2002 to ~2021 (1993-2002 ~0.3%, 1998-2007 ~-0.1%, 2003-12 ~-0.1%), Taiwan 2000-2020 ~1.0%, against Korea post-1997 at 2-3% and AMRO's (Jan 2024) judgment that China's deflationary risks are \"rising but not as severe as Japan's case in 1990s\".\nPRO-B CLUSTER (LR 1.45): China CPI +0.9% y/y Sept 2026 and +1.0% Jun 2026 (NBS via Mysteel); new home prices \"have fallen year-on-year for 38 consecutive months\"; property's share of household assets down \"from 67% in mid-2021 to 52%\"; household consumption ~39.6-40% of GDP with the 15th Five-Year Plan calling for raising the share but \"they have not set a specific target\"; Beijing \"has largely resisted economists' calls for fiscal stimulus\". Raw components would multiply to ~2.3; held to 1.45 for heavy overlap with the prior's own reference class.\nANTI-B CLUSTER (LR 0.61): China PPI +5.0% y/y Sept 2026 and +4.1% Jun 2026 (Mysteel), after \"the 37th straight month of decline\" as of Oct 2025, corroborated in direction by T. Rowe Price (Apr 2026), \"the oil price jump resulting from the Middle East conflict helped flip the change in PPI into positive territory in March 2026 given the close correlation of Chinese PPI and oil prices\", under the title \"As China's Disinflation Fades, Inflation Risks May Rise Globally\"; plus anti-involution capacity consolidation as an explicitly price-RAISING policy and the locked 2A demographic flip (population -60M 2026-35, worker/retiree 4.4 -> 2.8) turning China from saving-glut deflation exporter to labour-scarce dissaver in the back half. MY MAIN DISAGREEMENT WITH THE SCOUT, stated as an LR: the Scout calls the PPI sign flip \"the single most under-priced signal\", but China has repeatedly run high PPI alongside sub-1.5% CPI — PPI averaged ~+6.3% in 2017 with CPI +1.6%, and ~+8.1% in 2021 with CPI +0.9% — so P(PPI spike | CPI stays low) is HIGH, not low, and the LR is accordingly weak (~0.75), before the Scout's own caveat that the +5.0% level is single-source: \"I couldn't find any source reporting China's September 2026 PPI at +5%, so I can't confirm that figure.\" I also keep the analyst caveat verbatim: \"PPI will stay under pressure until the property market stabilizes and consumer confidence recovers\", adding \"the campaign alone is unlikely to change the trend.\" DISCOUNTED to 0.85 for the PPI leg; the demographic-flip leg carries 0.85 on its own as structure (Fermi's split window: 4 years at ~0.8% plus 6 at ~1.9% = 1.46%, knife-edge just under). Net anti-cluster 0.61... held at 0.61.\nPosterior B odds = 1.381 x 1.45 x 0.61 = 1.222 → CONJUNCT B = 0.55.\n\nCORRELATION: -0.10. Negative channel: a China export-price fall of 3%/yr at 30% pass-through into a ~21-23%-weight core-goods basket is -0.19pp/yr on OECD core, so B true makes A harder. Positive channel: tariff/fragmentation walls raise OECD goods prices while diverted export supply stays home and gluts China's domestic market — exactly the locked 2A regime, and exactly the joint cell asked for. The wall is leaky and a common BIS-Box-C demand shortfall drags both down together, so small net negative rather than the Historian's -0.20.\nJOINT = 0.62 x 0.55 + (-0.10) x sqrt(0.62x0.38x0.55x0.45) = 0.341 - 0.10x0.2415 = 0.317 → 32.\nUPDATE DIRECTION: AMPLIFIED toward YES, 14 → 32 (+18), the largest move in my seat. Decomposition of the +18: +15 from the structural correction to conjunct A (omitted high-inflation OECD-38 members at ~8.8% PPP weight contributing ~0.55-0.62pp, which is the entire difference between 1.95% and 2.53%), +4 from the hard core-PCE/Destatis/INE telemetry, -1 net from conjunct B and the correlation. Only ~4 of the 18 points are EVIDENCE; the rest is arithmetic the prior omitted, and a reader should discount my CQ2 accordingly if they reject the EM-member weighting convention.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:56.844Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"Turkiye annual CPI inflation (TurkStat, December print) falls below 8.0% y/y for two consecutive calendar years, and Mexico's headline CPI (INEGI) averages below 3.5% over the same two years.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies the load-bearing plank of my CQ2 — that ~0.55-0.62pp of GDP-weighted OECD-38 core comes from five high-inflation members at ~8.8% weight. CQ2 falls from 32 to ~18-20 (conjunct A from 0.62 to ~0.42); CQ6-C falls from 22 to ~15 and CQ6-D rises from 12 to ~18; my CQ6 would converge on the Historian's prior, i.e. my whole +18 amplification would be shown to be an artifact of the weighting convention rather than a finding.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"US core PCE y/y (BEA) prints below 2.2% for six consecutive months while the euro-area HICP core (Eurostat) prints below 2.0% over the same window.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2029\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Kills the front-loading leg (2027-2030 at ~2.9%) that my CQ2 depends on to clear the 10-year 2.50% bar, since the back half would then need >=2.6% from a cold start. CQ2 falls to ~16; CQ6-C falls to ~12-14 and CQ6-D rises to ~20; the evidence cluster I assigned LR 2.2 would be shown to have been a level reading with no persistence, which is exactly the overconfidence failure my seat is supposed to avoid.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"China's annual headline CPI (NBS) prints at or above 2.0% in any two consecutive calendar years, with the services sub-index above the goods sub-index in both.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2032\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies my conjunct B at 0.55 and vindicates the Scout's PPI/anti-involution read that I discounted to LR 0.85. CQ2 falls from 32 to ~20 (B from 0.55 to ~0.30); it would also mean the demographic labour-scarcity channel in China is reflationary earlier than the locked 2A arithmetic implies, which raises my CQ6-C by ~3 and lowers CQ3's China leg's deflation-driven downside, nudging CQ3 P50 up ~0.1pp.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"ICE BofA MOVE calendar-year average exceeds 110 in both 2028 and 2029, with the G7 GDP-weighted 10y nominal yield averaging above 4.5% in those years.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2029\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Confirms the level-scaling structure I used to raise CQ5's conjunct A from 0.283 to 0.35 and would make the compressed-start evidence (MOVE ~59-71 in late 2025 / 2026, LR 0.80 after overlap haircut) worthless. CQ5 rises from 26 toward ~38; my CQ4 P50 moves up ~0.3pp toward 2.45 and the P90 toward 3.8; CQ6-C gains ~4 at B's expense. The reverse — MOVE averaging below 85 across 2027-2029 — drops CQ5 to ~15.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Eurostat Structure of Earnings Survey (the 2030 wave, published ~2032-33) shows real median hourly earnings growth in ISIC Q exceeding ISIC J by more than 5.0pp cumulatively since the 2026 wave, in at least five of the EU's largest member states.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2033\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies the two mechanisms carrying my CQ1 down to 8 — administered-price suppression of care wages and the median-vs-share composition trap (LRs 0.70 and 0.60). CQ1 rises from 8 to ~22-25 (conjunct 2 from 0.11 to ~0.35); it also raises CQ6-C by ~3 via the care-wage channel into core services, and raises my score on hypothesis plank (a) from 5 to ~20. This is the cheapest available test of my seat's largest downward update, and it resolves on an administrative release rather than on telemetry.\"}","updateBasis":{"kind":"launch","note":"run2 wave 2 bayes seat, effort 'inflation'."}}